Use `if` at the end of a template head to include one piece of output. A
false condition leaves that piece out. A surrounding template can still
produce its own text.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    changed = true
    status = [:Build complete[if changed: with changes].]
]

With `changed` set to `true`, the result reads `Build complete with changes.`.
Set it to `false` and the result reads `Build complete.`. To include a value
only when an option contains it, use an option capture.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    maybe_name String? = "Priya"
    message = [:Welcome[if maybe_name is |name|:, [name]]!]
]

Use `loop` to make one contribution per iteration. This function collects
each greeting as a separate String. The inner template puts the name into
the greeting. The outer template collects the results in order.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greetings |names [String]| -> [String] {
        return [loop names |name|:
            [:Hello, [name]!]
        ]
    }
]

Template conditions select one body. For alternative bodies or an early loop
exit, use ordinary @../branching (branching) or @../loops (loops).
